Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hi Jun,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1:

We propose a framework where a user can select one of two categories of
cloud storage options. We describe a failover approach for each. Given the
fundamental differences between multi-AZ and single-AZ cloud storage, it is
not feasible for the algorithm to be the same, but we can support both as
described below:

Multi-AZ cloud storage (a user is prioritizing availability, e.g. S3):
When a leader or AZ fails, the multi-AZ cloud storage remains available. A
new leader can be elected, and that leader can begin reading from S3. The
followers can also read from S3 to catch up. And followers can also perform
replica reconciliation and truncation locally as needed.

Single-AZ cloud storage (a user is prioritizing latency, e.g., S3E1Z):
When leader B1 fails or B1's S3E1Z AZ1 fails, a new leader B2 is elected in
AZ2. The new leader B2 will upload to AZ2 S3E1Z for new produce requests as
they are received. For the fetch requests of the followers asking for older
data, it will respond with data over standard kafka replication (i.e. from
its local disk).

"Neither approach addresses the availability issue when a partition is
reassigned to a new replica and an S3EZ1 is down."

Assume AZ1 for S3E1Z is down, and we lose previous leader B1. Then we elect
a new leader B2 in AZ2.
We also provision a new replica B4 in AZ3, it builds its log in two phases:
1. Historical Catch up: First B4 downloads all the inactive historical
segments from tiered storage (KIP-405), this is multi-AZ storage always
available
2. Active Catch Up: After B4 has the full history, it contacts the new
leader B2 to get the most recent active data. It reads this data from S3E1Z
in AZ2.

After KIP-1023 is added the amount of data fetched directly from the leader
(step 2) will be reduced as we will read historical data starting at the
last-tiered-offset.

JR2: For partition reassignment, we can see it working this way:
Originally, we proposed embedding the AZ-replicator node in the metadata
message for that chunk of data. However, this leads to a static assignment
that may have difficulty with replica reassignment or losing nodes.
Instead, since we are switching to sending the metadata directly in the
fetch response, rather than a separate metadata topic (see JR8 below), then
the leader, with its view of ISRs and AZs, will dynamically determine which
node will be the replicator in each fetch response. This way we can handle
reassignment as nodes come up or die.

JR8:

"So, if there are more files actively accumulating data, the flush
frequency and therefore RPO is reduced."

As discussed with Luke, we will switch to the approach of embedding the
metadata within the existing log segment. This metadata can be sent
directly over the replication protocol. The data will be uploaded to cloud
storage and read by AZ-replicator nodes.

This avoids the problems of duplicate metadata partition/log segments. With
this approach:
1. We are not doubling partitions/log segments: partition count/file count
renames the same.
2. We have one active log segment per kafka partition (same as standard):
It holds the data and metadata
3. Since we haven't increased the number of active, dirty files, we do not
negatively impact the kernel's flush frequency or the IOPS of other topics.

Thanks,
Tom & Henry

