> Failover:
> We are open to this idea on using standard replication protocol. The logi=
c
> can work as follows.
> 1. B1 leader failure
> 2. B2 elected as new leader, has a higher LEO than the high watermark, it
> increments the leader epoch
> 3. B3 makes a fetch response(s) with the previous leader epoch
> 4. B2 responds with actual data in its responses (not the expected
> metadata-only responses), it continues to do so until B3 high watermark h=
as
> caught up
> 5. After high watermark of B3 has caught up, it sends typical metadata-on=
ly
> responses
>
> This allows us to have the same availability as a standard Kafka topic
> during failover since we use the standard replication protocol.
>
> If a new replacement broker B4 is created in place of B1, the leader can
> recognize that it has no prior state, and will return metadata-only
> responses. B1 will need to send all data not already in tiered storage to
> broker B4, so this could be a large amount of data, so for an efficient
> solution it needs to use metadata-only.
>
> The leader should be able to distinguish these two cases (follower mostly
> caught up during failover, vs completely new broker).
>
> JR8 Sidebar:
> I agree that ideally we want a design that does not require duplicate
> partitions. We originally considered sending metadata via the fetch
> response, but had discarded it due to the significant changes for
> replication protocol. However, based on sentiment in these threads, we wa=
nt
> the correct, scalable approach, even if it's more work. We are open to su=
ch
> an approach. Here's how we see it working
>
> 1. Leader B1 receives a produce request
> 2. B1 appends the produce request to its current log segment
> 3. A thread on B1 uploads the produce batch onto object storage async
> (possibly combined with other data of other partitions). The cloud storag=
e
> path is added to the current segment file of the partition (and any other
> partitions' log segments in the case of a combined object)
> 4. Follower broker B2 makes a fetch response for the topic with flag
> walMetadata=3Dtrue
> 5. B2 receives the object storage path metadata, and issues GET request t=
o
> cloud storage
>
> The key part is that we are storing the WAL metadata in the existing
> partition's log segment file. The metadata should be quite small (e.g.,
> tens of bytes) so it should not have a significant impact on the leader
> broker by tracking this as well. And there is no need for another file
> descriptor to be open. The main advantage here is that we get
> point-to-point communication of metadata to avoid metadata fanout.
>
> Is there anything you'd add or change to the above description?
>
> One alternative option we considered for metadata fanout is using the sam=
e
> approach we use for data mentioned in my previous reply to Jun: using an
> Intra-AZ replicator node, in this case for metadata. For example, with RF=
=3D3
> on the metadata topic, we have three intra-AZ replicator nodes for
> metadata. All nodes in each AZ read from that replicator node. Thus the
> metadata transfer is free. However, we do not prefer this solution, becau=
se
> it still means that all nodes are processing all metadata messages (since
> number partitions would be relatively small e.g., 50). So the transfer co=
st
> may be free, but compute/memory is spent by brokers filtering metadata.
> Based on this, we prefer the previously described solution (sending
> metadata directly to brokers, and reusing existing partition log segments=
).
>
> Thanks for the insightful discussion,
> Tom
