Hi, Tom,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1. Ok, I can see an approach that could make this work. This is the rough
outline.

1. A new leader is elected.
2. Leader maintains a first tiered offset, which is initialized to log end
offset.
3. Leader writes produced data from the client to local log.
4. Leader uploads produced data from all local logs as a combined object
5. Leader stores the metadata for the combined object in memory.
6. If a follower fetch request has an offset >=3D first tiered offset, the
metadata for the corresponding combined object is returned. Otherwise, the
local data is returned.
7. Leader periodically advances first tiered offset.

This works for most cases. The only issue is that when S3EZ1 in the leader
zone fails, the leader could still be alive and thus writes are blocked.
One option is to trigger
a leader election in that case. This requires more logic in the controller
and also doesn't prevent the leader from switching back due to preferred
leader election. Another option is
to always write each combined object to 2 S3EZ1 in two different zones.
This way, data in S3EZ1 is always available, which allows us to use the
same approach for S3 and S3EZ1.

JR2. "then the leader, with its view of ISRs and AZs, will dynamically
determine which
node will be the replicator in each fetch response."

I am not sure if this is enough. We need at least a way to control replica
assignment when the topic is originally created. Perhaps we could have a
new assignment policy for the new topic type. We should also consider
whether we need to handle the existing partition reassignment API
differently.

Jun


