Hi Jun,

Thanks for the reply. We are glad we are converging on a design.

JR1: Data Flow & S3E1Z Availability
We agree with your 7-step outline. There is one clarification on Step 5
("Leader stores the metadata [...] in memory"):

To ensure durability and prevent metadata loss if the leader crashes, we
will persist this metadata into the leader's local log segment (using the
file-based cache) rather than just holding it in memory. We can use header
info to store this metadata. Specifically, we propose adding new header
fields to the DefaultRecordBatch class to store the S3 path/location. When
the FetchResponse is received, we utilize the original file-based cache
mechanism to serve it. This gives us the persistence we need without
re-introducing the RPO/IOPS problem.

Regarding the case of a live leader with a failed S3E1Z, we agree with your
second solution: always write each object to 2 S3E1Z zones.

As you noted, this makes the data in S3E1Z inherently available (tolerating
a single AZ failure) and, most importantly, allows us to use the same
unified replication logic for both S3 and S3E1Z. This removes the need for
the complex, dual-path failover logic. We will update the KIP to adopt this
as the new high-availability model for S3E1Z.

We acknowledge this will increase the producer cost (twice the write
operations). However, read costs remain the same since data only needs to
be read from one S3E1Z zone. Presumably, if a user selects S3E1Z over S3,
they are willing to bear this write cost for the latency benefits.

JR2: Replica Assignment & Separating Optimizations
We agree that we need a new, AZ-aware assignment policy for this topic
type. This policy would be the default for these topics and would ensure
that replicas are intelligently distributed across different availability
zones at topic creation time.

However, regarding the cost optimizations we discussed previously
(specifically the Intra-AZ Replicator for the read path and the Combined
Object logic for the producer/write path), we propose moving these
mechanisms to a separate KIP.

Separating these optimizations allows KIP-1176 to focus on the core
architecture=E2=80=94enabling the S3E1Z integration, the unified replicatio=
n
protocol, and the HA model=E2=80=94without the added complexity of the
cost-optimization layer. We can address the "Producer Cost" and "Read Path
Optimization" (AZ-Replicators) in a follow-up proposal.

Thanks,
Tom

